+# Hunt report: export worker acceptance and restart recovery
+
+Revision: `6b84ce8` (working tree adds only `tests/hunt_witness.py` and this
+report). Python 3.9.6, SQLite 3.43.2. Date: 2026-10-24.
+
+## Scope and stopping condition
+
+Surface: `Queue.submit`, `Queue.work`, `Queue.recover` / `Queue.__init__`
+and `Queue.result` in `app/export_queue.py`, under the deployment in
+`app/export-contract.md`. Governing claim:
+[CLAIM-export-recovery](specs/CLAIM-export-recovery.md) (current verdict
+`falsified`, witness `tests/known_loss.py`). `app/exporter.py` is note export
+via `Store`, not this queue, and is out of scope.
+
+Prioritized harms: accepted work abandoned (the contract's main obligation),
+false acceptance, lost or duplicated results, permanently stuck jobs.
+Stopping condition: every failure point in `submit`/`work` that the contract
+permits (process termination, transient I/O failure followed by the
+application retrying `work`, restart) checked against "accepted jobs
+eventually produce their result."
+
+## Fault model (from `app/export-contract.md`)
+
+- Committed SQLite transactions survive termination. Storage loss and
+  corruption are excluded.
+- One worker process, no overlapping calls. Restart happens only after the
+  previous worker has stopped.
+- After transient I/O failures the application retries, and it calls `work`
+  repeatedly once the environment recovers. **This covers in-process retry
+  as well as restart.**
+- A job ID has one payload throughout its lifetime. I read this as a premise
+  on callers (see conditional concern C1).
+- The `after_claim` hook marks a real interruption point after the
+  `running` commit.
+
+## Existing finding (reproduced)
+
+**F0 — restart does not reclaim `running` jobs.** `tests/known_loss.py`
+interrupts at `after_claim`, restarts, and calls `work`. `job-1` stays
+`running` because `work` selects only `state = 'pending'` and `recover()` is
+a no-op. Rerun today: `python3 tests/known_loss.py` → `AssertionError:
+accepted work abandoned after restart`, exit 1. Still unresolved, and the
+claim verdict stays `falsified`.
+
+## New findings (demonstrated defects)
+
+Both have the same root cause as F0: nothing ever turns `running` back into
+claimable work. They are separate reachable paths, and a fix that only covers
+restart does not close them.
+
+**F1 — abandoned job after a transient failure with in-process retry (no
+restart).**
+- Trace: `work` commits `running` (export_queue.py:33-34). An exception at
+  the `after_claim` boundary (line 35-36) propagates out. The contract has the
+  same process retry `work`. The `pending`-only SELECT (line 28-30) never sees
+  the job again, so `work` returns `False` and `result("job-1")` stays `None`
+  for the life of the process.
+- Harm: the accepted job is abandoned until a restart, and even then F0
+  applies. Under a recover-only fix the job is still stuck for as long as the
+  process runs, which is unbounded.
+- Witness: `tests/hunt_witness.py`, `case_hook_retry_without_restart`.
+
+**F2 — a failure in the completion transaction strands the job, without the
+test hook.**
+- Trace: the completion `with self.db:` block (lines 37-39) is atomic. If the
+  `INSERT INTO results` (or its commit) fails, the transaction rolls back to
+  the already-committed `running` state. That leaves the same stranded job as
+  F0/F1, reached through a production statement failure instead of the
+  instrumentation hook.
+- Witness: `case_completion_failure` uses a SQLite authorizer to deny the
+  results INSERT once, as a stand-in for a transient SQLITE_FULL/IOERR. It then
+  lets the environment recover, drains `work` in-process, and restarts and
+  drains again. Observed: `result=None` both in-process and after restart.
+- Caveat: the authorizer denial is a substitute for a real I/O error. It
+  shows that any statement error at that point leaves the job `running`. It
+  does not reproduce a disk-full condition.
+
+### Witness
+
+```
+python3 tests/hunt_witness.py
+FAIL case_hook_retry_without_restart: in-process retry: result=None
+FAIL case_completion_failure: completion failure: in-process result=None, after restart result=None
+exit 1
+```
+
+Exit 0 means every accepted job produced its result. Exit 1 means at least
+one case violated the contract.
+
+Oracle checks, run on scratch copies of `app/export_queue.py` outside the
+repo and then deleted. Product code was not modified:
+
+| Variant | known_loss.py | hunt_witness.py |
+|---|---|---|
+| Current code | exit 1 | exit 1 (A FAIL, B FAIL) |
+| `recover()` resets `running`→`pending` at startup only | exit 0 | exit 1 (A FAIL; B FAIL in-process, PASS after restart) |
+| `work` selects `state IN ('pending','running')` | exit 0 | exit 0 |
+
+So the existing witness alone would accept a restart-only repair that still
+abandons accepted work under the contract's in-process retry.
+
+### Fix direction (not applied)
+
+Make `running` rows reclaimable by every later `work` call, not just at
+startup. With one worker and no overlapping calls, any `running` row that a
+new `work` call sees belongs to an abandoned attempt. One option is to select
+`state IN ('pending','running')`. Another is to reset the row to `pending`
+when an exception leaves `work`, combined with a startup reset for crashes.
+Re-execution is safe from duplicate results because the results INSERT and
+the `done` update commit together. If the reset happens in the exception
+path, a crash before the reset still needs the startup reset.
+
+## Conditional concern
+
+**C1 — resubmitting an ID with a different payload reports success but is
+ignored.** `submit` uses `INSERT OR IGNORE` and always returns
+`{"accepted": True}`. The contract states "a job ID has one payload throughout
+its lifetime." If that is a caller premise, this behavior is correct
+idempotent resubmission. If it is a system obligation, the second payload
+gets a false acceptance. This is a requirements question, not a demonstrated
+defect.
+
+## Checked without counterexample
+
+- **Durability of acceptance:** `submit` returns only after its `with
+  self.db` commit. A commit failure raises instead of returning accepted.
+- **Failure in the claim transaction** (`UPDATE ... 'running'`): the
+  rollback leaves the job `pending`, so a retry picks it up (code reading).
+- **Crash after the completion commit:** the result and `done` are both
+  durable. `work` never selects `done` rows, so the job is not redone.
+- **Duplicate results:** the results INSERT and the `done` update are in
+  one transaction, so a committed result always means `done` (code reading).
+- **Idempotent resubmission with the same payload**, including after
+  completion: ignored, and the existing result is kept (code reading).
+- **Repeated `work` calls:** each committed `done` removes a job from
+  selection, so pending jobs drain under the one-worker premise.
+
+## Gaps and untested conditions
+
+- Real I/O errors (disk full, EIO, SQLITE_BUSY from an external reader) were
+  not injected. F2 uses an authorizer denial instead.
+- `KeyboardInterrupt` or signals delivered inside a `with self.db` block, and
+  Python `sqlite3` rollback behavior when commit itself fails, were analysed
+  only by reading the code.
+- Multi-worker and overlapping-call behavior is outside the contract and was
+  not examined.
+- Durability rests on SQLite defaults (rollback journal, `synchronous=FULL`)
+  being in effect. This was not checked against deployment configuration,
+  because the contract states it as a premise.
+- No claim record, proof, or verification file was changed. Under the claims
+  workflow, F1 and F2 are further counterexamples to
+  [CLAIM-export-recovery](specs/CLAIM-export-recovery.md) and could be linked
+  from its evidence alongside `tests/known_loss.py` when that evidence is next
+  updated. Any repair must clear F0, F1 and F2 independently.
